* Test with real benchmarks (BugsC++)
* Better way to handle namespaces for C++
* 3rd-party FL tools
* A patch cannot call an unreferenced `static` function
  A callee is resolved by name at run time now (dlsym, from function-info.json), and a static
  function that nothing in its own file references is never emitted by the compiler at all, so
  the lookup finds nothing and the object is skipped. Taking its address is what used to force
  it into the binary. Fix, if experiments show it is worth it: have metapro emit, in the file
  that defines it, a uniquely named exported pointer to it, e.g.
      void* const __metapro_fn_<file>__<name> = (void*)&<name>;
  which forces the compiler to emit the function, is always link-safe because the definition is
  right there, and gives it an unambiguous name (two files may each define a static of the same
  name, and both land in .symtab as local symbols). Verified in a prototype, including that
  -Wl,--gc-sections collects those pointers again unless the program is linked with -rdynamic.
* Macro expansion uses macros of the whole project, not of the patched file (ffmpeg-42525124)
  macros.json is one table for the program, but a #define is only in effect in the translation
  units that see it, so _expand_macros() in gen-san2patch-config-ast.py can rewrite a name with a
  definition the patched file never had. ffmpeg's `get_bits_left(gb)` in libavformat/jpegxl_probe.c
  became `bits_left_be(gb)`, through
      get_bits_left -> bits_left   (libavcodec/get_bits.h)
      bits_left     -> bits_left_be (libavcodec/bitstream.h)
  which are the new bitstream reader's, while that file gets the classic reader, where
  get_bits_left is a plain `static inline` function (preprocessing it shows the definition and no
  bits_left_be anywhere). Both names are in function-info.json, collected from whichever unit
  defines each, so the runtime accepts the rewritten one and then finds no symbol of it:
      function.c: ERROR: Function bits_left_be not found in variables and not in the program
  Fix, cheapest first:
  - do not rewrite a name that is itself a function of the program: for an object-like macro whose
    body is a bare identifier (a rename), leave the call alone when the original name is in
    function-info.json. Narrow, and covers this failure mode.
  - make the table per file: the collector already records the file each macro is defined in, so
    emitting macros.json per instrumented translation unit (or recording each unit's include
    closure) makes expansion use only the definitions actually in effect.
